//! Subscriptions: the client-chosen `sid`, the table it indexes, and
//! auto-unsubscribe.
//!
//! # The `sid` is the client's
//!
//! "`SUB <subject> [queue group] <sid>` — a unique alphanumeric subscription
//! ID, generated by the client." So the server never invents one and never
//! validates one beyond uniqueness on the connection: it is a routing token
//! the client hands out and the server quotes back in every `MSG`. This crate
//! allocates them as a monotonically increasing decimal `u64`, which is
//! unique for the life of a connection by construction and needs no lookup to
//! produce.
//!
//! A consequence worth stating: because the client picks them, **nothing but
//! the client can bound the table**. Each entry is a live channel, a subject
//! and a queue-group name, and the protocol says nothing about how many there
//! may be. [`ConnectionOptions::max_subscriptions`](crate::options::ConnectionOptions::max_subscriptions)
//! is that bound and it is ours.
//!
//! # `UNSUB` with a count is honoured twice
//!
//! `UNSUB <sid> [max_msgs]` is "a number of messages to wait for before
//! automatically unsubscribing", and the count is the *total* delivered on
//! that subscription, not a further N: a subscription that has had three and
//! is given `UNSUB sid 3` is done immediately. The server keeps that count
//! and so does this client's own table, and that is not redundancy — the
//! server may well write the Nth `MSG` before it reads the `UNSUB`, and it
//! certainly writes any message already in flight. A client that only sent
//! the count would deliver messages past the one the caller asked to stop at.
//!
//! The server counts every `MSG` it writes on the `sid`, whether or not this
//! client could queue the copy, so the table counts the same thing: a copy
//! dropped for want of room (below) still advances the count. A table that
//! counted only what it queued would stay behind the server for ever after
//! one drop, and a subscription the server has already ended would never end
//! here.
//!
//! # A full queue drops the copy
//!
//! Each subscription has a bounded queue and a message that arrives for a
//! full one is dropped and counted. The alternative — blocking the read loop
//! until the application collects — stalls every *other* subscription on the
//! connection and then earns a slow-consumer disconnect from the server
//! anyway, since the server buffers only `max_pending` (64 MiB) per client
//! before dropping it (`docs/research/nats.md` §5, §11). Core NATS is
//! at-most-once and stores nothing for replay, so a dropped copy is a loss
//! the delivery contract already allows; a stalled connection is not.
//!
//! There are two ways a queue is full: it holds `subscription_queue`
//! messages, or the connection as a whole holds `max_queued_bytes` of
//! delivered messages that nobody has collected (`crate::queue`). A message
//! count is not a memory bound, so both are checked, and both end the same
//! way. [`Subscription::dropped`] says how many copies this subscription has
//! lost; the log says so too, but at most once a second per subscription,
//! because the peer decides how fast messages arrive and an overloaded client
//! that logged every loss would spend what it has left on the log.
//!
//! # A subscription that is gone is found out
//!
//! Dropping a [`Subscription`] sends an `UNSUB`, but a `Drop` cannot wait for
//! room in a full command queue, so the `UNSUB` can be lost. The driver does
//! not rely on it: an entry whose reader has gone is swept — on every ping, and
//! before the table refuses a subscription for being full — and the `UNSUB`
//! written from there.

use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
use std::sync::{Arc, OnceLock};
use std::time::Instant;

use tokio::sync::mpsc;

use crate::connection::Command;
use crate::message::Message;
use crate::queue::{Budget, Queued};
use crate::refusal::{Named, Refusal};
use crate::report::ReportLog;

/// What a table entry and the [`Subscription`] handed to the caller share.
#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub(crate) struct Shared {
    /// Copies dropped.
    dropped: AtomicU64,
    /// The refusal that ended the subscription, where the server refused it.
    refusal: OnceLock<Refusal>,
}

/// One live subscription.
///
/// Dropping it unsubscribes: the handle is the subscription's lifetime, which
/// is what keeps the table from filling with interest nobody reads. The
/// `UNSUB` is best-effort on the drop path, because a `Drop` cannot await and
/// a connection that is already gone has no subscriptions to remove anyway;
/// the driver sweeps up after any that are lost.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Subscription {
    sid: u64,
    subject: Vec<u8>,
    queue_group: Option<Vec<u8>>,
    messages: mpsc::Receiver<Queued>,
    commands: mpsc::Sender<Command>,
    /// Shared with this subscription's table entry.
    shared: Arc<Shared>,
    /// Set once an `UNSUB` has been asked for, so `Drop` does not send a
    /// second one.
    released: bool,
}

impl Subscription {
    pub(crate) fn new(
        sid: u64,
        subject: Vec<u8>,
        queue_group: Option<Vec<u8>>,
        messages: mpsc::Receiver<Queued>,
        commands: mpsc::Sender<Command>,
        shared: Arc<Shared>,
    ) -> Self {
        Self {
            sid,
            subject,
            queue_group,
            messages,
            commands,
            shared,
            released: false,
        }
    }

    /// The `sid` this client chose, which is what every `MSG` for this
    /// subscription carries.
    #[must_use]
    pub const fn sid(&self) -> u64 {
        self.sid
    }

    /// The subject or pattern this subscription asked for.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn subject(&self) -> &[u8] {
        &self.subject
    }

    /// The queue group, where this subscription joined one.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn queue_group(&self) -> Option<&[u8]> {
        self.queue_group.as_deref()
    }

    /// How many messages addressed to this subscription were dropped because
    /// it could not take them: its queue was full, or the connection already
    /// held [`max_queued_bytes`](crate::options::ConnectionOptions::max_queued_bytes)
    /// of messages waiting for readers.
    ///
    /// Core NATS is at-most-once, so a reader that falls behind loses copies
    /// rather than stalling the connection; this is how much it has lost.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn dropped(&self) -> u64 {
        self.shared.dropped.load(Ordering::Relaxed)
    }

    /// The refusal that ended this subscription, where the server refused it.
    ///
    /// `Some` once the server has answered the `SUB` with a permissions
    /// violation naming this subscription: the server holds no such
    /// subscription, so no message will ever arrive, and [`next`](Self::next)
    /// has ended. `None` for a subscription that was not refused, including
    /// one that ended some other way. Refusals the server does not tie to a
    /// subscription are on [`Connection::refusals`](crate::Connection::refusals)
    /// only.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn refusal(&self) -> Option<&Refusal> {
        self.shared.refusal.get()
    }

    /// The next message, or `None` once the subscription has ended.
    ///
    /// Ends when the subscription is unsubscribed, when an auto-unsubscribe
    /// count is reached — as the *server* counts it, so a copy this client
    /// had no room for counts too — when the server refuses the subscription
    /// ([`refusal`](Self::refusal)), or when the connection goes away: a Core
    /// NATS subscription has no existence apart from its connection.
    pub async fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Message> {
        self.messages.recv().await.map(Queued::into_message)
    }

    /// The next message if one is already waiting.
    pub fn try_next(&mut self) -> Option<Message> {
        self.messages.try_recv().ok().map(Queued::into_message)
    }

    /// `UNSUB <sid>`: remove the subscription now.
    ///
    /// Messages the server had already written are still in the queue and
    /// still readable, because they were delivered before the `UNSUB` could
    /// be read. Dropping them would be pretending the `UNSUB` travelled back
    /// in time.
    pub async fn unsubscribe(&mut self) {
        self.request_unsubscribe(None).await;
    }

    /// `UNSUB <sid> <max_msgs>`: remove the subscription once it has received
    /// `max_msgs` messages in total.
    ///
    /// The count is the total on this subscription, as the server counts it,
    /// so a subscription that has already had that many ends at once.
    pub async fn unsubscribe_after(&mut self, max_msgs: u64) {
        self.request_unsubscribe(Some(max_msgs)).await;
    }

    async fn request_unsubscribe(&mut self, max_msgs: Option<u64>) {
        self.released = true;
        let _ = self
            .commands
            .send(Command::Unsubscribe {
                sid: self.sid,
                max_msgs,
            })
            .await;
    }
}

impl Drop for Subscription {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if self.released {
            return;
        }
        // Best-effort: a `Drop` cannot await, and a driver that is gone or
        // whose queue is full has either ended the subscription already or
        // will find the closed channel when it next sweeps the table.
        let _ = self.commands.try_send(Command::Unsubscribe {
            sid: self.sid,
            max_msgs: None,
        });
    }
}

/// Why a copy was dropped.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum DropReason {
    /// The subscription already held `subscription_queue` messages.
    QueueFull,
    /// The connection already held `max_queued_bytes` of messages waiting for
    /// readers.
    OverBudget,
}

/// What one delivery attempt did.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum Delivered {
    /// Handed to the subscription. `exhausted` means an auto-unsubscribe
    /// count was reached and the entry is gone.
    Ok {
        /// Whether this was the last message the subscription asked for.
        exhausted: bool,
    },
    /// The copy was dropped and counted. `report` is set where it is time to
    /// say so again, and `None` where it is not.
    Dropped {
        /// Why.
        reason: DropReason,
        /// Set at most once a second per subscription.
        report: Option<DropReport>,
    },
    /// Nobody is reading any more; the entry has been removed and an `UNSUB`
    /// is due.
    Gone,
    /// No subscription has that `sid`.
    Unknown,
}

/// What a log line about dropped copies says.
///
/// Carried with the answer because the entry the copy was dropped for may be
/// gone by the time the caller looks: a drop can be the copy that reaches an
/// auto-unsubscribe count.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) struct DropReport {
    /// How many copies were dropped since the last time this subscription's
    /// drops were reported, this one included.
    pub(crate) dropped: u64,
    /// What the subscription asked for.
    pub(crate) subject: Vec<u8>,
}

/// One entry of the subscription table.
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Entry {
    subject: Vec<u8>,
    queue_group: Option<Vec<u8>>,
    tx: mpsc::Sender<Queued>,
    /// How many `MSG`s the server has sent on this `sid`, which is the number
    /// `UNSUB <sid> <max_msgs>` counts. Every one counts, a copy that was
    /// dropped for want of room included, because the server counted it when
    /// it wrote it.
    received: u64,
    max_msgs: Option<u64>,
    /// What the [`Subscription`] holds the other end of: the copies dropped
    /// and the refusal, where there was one.
    shared: Arc<Shared>,
    log: ReportLog,
}

/// The subscription table, owned by the connection driver.
///
/// A `HashMap` keyed by `sid`, bounded by a number of ours. One owner: the
/// driver both dispatches into it and edits it, so there is no lock and no
/// window in which a `MSG` can arrive for a subscription that is half
/// inserted.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub(crate) struct Table {
    entries: HashMap<u64, Entry>,
    max: usize,
}

impl Table {
    pub(crate) fn new(max: usize) -> Self {
        Self {
            entries: HashMap::new(),
            max,
        }
    }

    /// Whether another subscription would fit.
    pub(crate) fn has_room(&self) -> bool {
        self.entries.len() < self.max
    }

    /// The bound, for the error that reports it.
    pub(crate) const fn max(&self) -> usize {
        self.max
    }

    /// Adds a subscription and returns what it shares with the
    /// [`Subscription`] handed to the caller.
    pub(crate) fn insert(
        &mut self,
        sid: u64,
        subject: Vec<u8>,
        queue_group: Option<Vec<u8>>,
        tx: mpsc::Sender<Queued>,
    ) -> Arc<Shared> {
        let shared = Arc::new(Shared::default());
        self.entries.insert(
            sid,
            Entry {
                subject,
                queue_group,
                tx,
                received: 0,
                max_msgs: None,
                shared: Arc::clone(&shared),
                log: ReportLog::default(),
            },
        );
        shared
    }

    /// Removes a subscription. `true` where there was one to remove, which is
    /// what decides whether an `UNSUB` is worth writing.
    pub(crate) fn remove(&mut self, sid: u64) -> bool {
        self.entries.remove(&sid).is_some()
    }

    /// Records an auto-unsubscribe count.
    ///
    /// `false` where the subscription no longer exists. The entry is removed
    /// immediately where the server has already sent that many, because the
    /// count is a total and not a further N.
    pub(crate) fn set_max_msgs(&mut self, sid: u64, max_msgs: u64) -> bool {
        let Some(entry) = self.entries.get_mut(&sid) else {
            return false;
        };
        if entry.received >= max_msgs {
            self.entries.remove(&sid);
            return true;
        }
        entry.max_msgs = Some(max_msgs);
        true
    }

    /// Hands `message` to the subscription that asked for it.
    ///
    /// Never to any other: the `sid` is the routing key and a message whose
    /// `sid` is not in the table is dropped rather than matched against
    /// subjects. A client that re-matched subjects here would be second
    /// guessing the server's own routing, and would deliver a copy to a
    /// subscription whose `UNSUB` had already been processed.
    ///
    /// The message is charged against `budget` before it is queued; where it
    /// does not fit, or the queue is full, it is dropped and counted.
    ///
    /// Every copy that reaches an entry counts toward its auto-unsubscribe
    /// count, the dropped ones too: the server counted each of them when it
    /// wrote it, and an entry that counted only what it queued would never
    /// reach the count the server already has.
    pub(crate) fn dispatch(
        &mut self,
        sid: u64,
        message: Message,
        budget: &Arc<Budget>,
    ) -> Delivered {
        let Some(entry) = self.entries.get_mut(&sid) else {
            return Delivered::Unknown;
        };
        entry.received += 1;
        let exhausted = entry.max_msgs.is_some_and(|max| entry.received >= max);
        let delivered = match budget.try_charge(message.queued_len()) {
            None => entry.record_drop(DropReason::OverBudget),
            Some(charge) => match entry.tx.try_send(Queued::new(message, charge)) {
                Ok(()) => Delivered::Ok { exhausted },
                // The message, and the charge it carried, are dropped with
                // the error.
                Err(mpsc::error::TrySendError::Full(_)) => entry.record_drop(DropReason::QueueFull),
                Err(mpsc::error::TrySendError::Closed(_)) => {
                    self.entries.remove(&sid);
                    return Delivered::Gone;
                }
            },
        };
        // The server has ended the subscription: dropping the sender is what
        // lets the reader take what is queued and then see the end.
        if exhausted {
            self.entries.remove(&sid);
        }
        delivered
    }

    /// Removes every entry whose reader has gone, returning their `sid`s so
    /// the caller can write the `UNSUB`s that are owed.
    ///
    /// The backstop for a `Subscription` whose own `UNSUB` was lost: a
    /// `Drop` cannot wait for room in a full command queue.
    pub(crate) fn take_closed(&mut self) -> Vec<u64> {
        let gone: Vec<u64> = self
            .entries
            .iter()
            .filter(|(_, entry)| entry.tx.is_closed())
            .map(|(sid, _)| *sid)
            .collect();
        for sid in &gone {
            self.entries.remove(sid);
        }
        gone
    }

    /// Ends every subscription `named` says the server has refused or
    /// removed, telling each one why, and returns how many there were.
    ///
    /// A `sid` is exact. Without one the server has only named a subject and
    /// a queue group, and every subscription asking for exactly that is
    /// ended: the server decided on the subject and the group, so it refused
    /// each of them alike. The server holds none of these any more, so no
    /// `UNSUB` is owed for them, and dropping their senders is what lets a
    /// reader take what is queued and then see the end.
    pub(crate) fn refuse(&mut self, named: &Named, refusal: &Refusal) -> usize {
        let hit: Vec<u64> = self
            .entries
            .iter()
            .filter(|(sid, entry)| match named.sid {
                Some(exact) => **sid == exact,
                None => entry.subject == named.subject && entry.queue_group == named.queue_group,
            })
            .map(|(sid, _)| *sid)
            .collect();
        for sid in &hit {
            if let Some(entry) = self.entries.remove(sid) {
                // The first refusal is the reason; a second one for the same
                // subscription changes nothing.
                let _ = entry.shared.refusal.set(refusal.clone());
            }
        }
        hit.len()
    }

    /// Closes every subscription, which is what the end of the connection
    /// does to them: "core subscriptions vanish with the connection and have
    /// no stored session".
    pub(crate) fn clear(&mut self) {
        self.entries.clear();
    }
}

impl Entry {
    /// Counts a drop and says whether this one is due a log line.
    fn record_drop(&mut self, reason: DropReason) -> Delivered {
        self.shared.dropped.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        let report = self.log.record(Instant::now()).map(|dropped| DropReport {
            dropped,
            subject: self.subject.clone(),
        });
        Delivered::Dropped { reason, report }
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    fn message(sid: u64) -> Message {
        Message {
            sid,
            subject: bytes::Bytes::from_static(b"a.b"),
            ..Message::default()
        }
    }

    /// A budget that is not the thing under test.
    fn roomy() -> Arc<Budget> {
        Budget::new(usize::MAX / 2)
    }

    fn received(rx: &mut mpsc::Receiver<Queued>) -> Message {
        rx.try_recv().expect("delivered").into_message()
    }

    /// A `MSG` goes to the subscription its `sid` names and to no other, even
    /// where both subscriptions match the same subject.
    #[test]
    fn a_message_reaches_only_the_sid_that_asked() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx1, mut rx1) = mpsc::channel(4);
        let (tx2, mut rx2) = mpsc::channel(4);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx1);
        table.insert(2, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx2);

        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert_eq!(received(&mut rx1).sid, 1);
        assert!(
            rx2.try_recv().is_err(),
            "the other subscription matches the same subject and still gets nothing"
        );
        assert_eq!(table.dispatch(9, message(9), &budget), Delivered::Unknown);
    }

    /// The count is a total, honoured locally: the fourth message on a
    /// subscription capped at three is not delivered even though the server
    /// wrote it.
    #[test]
    fn an_auto_unsubscribe_count_is_a_total_and_is_honoured_locally() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(8);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);

        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(table.set_max_msgs(1, 3), "the subscription is still there");
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: true },
            "the third is the last one asked for"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Unknown,
            "a fourth message the server had already written is not delivered"
        );
        let mut seen = 0;
        while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {
            seen += 1;
        }
        assert_eq!(seen, 3);
    }

    /// A count that is already satisfied ends the subscription at once, which
    /// is what "the total delivered" means.
    #[test]
    fn a_count_already_reached_ends_the_subscription_immediately() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, _rx) = mpsc::channel(8);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);
        table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget);
        table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget);
        assert!(table.set_max_msgs(1, 2));
        assert_eq!(table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget), Delivered::Unknown);
        assert!(
            !table.set_max_msgs(1, 5),
            "and there is nothing left to cap"
        );
    }

    /// The server counts every `MSG` it writes on the `sid`, so a copy this
    /// client had no room for still advances the count: with the queue full
    /// the subscription ends when the server's count says it has, and its
    /// reader sees the end rather than waiting for a message that will never
    /// come.
    #[test]
    fn a_dropped_copy_still_counts_toward_the_auto_unsubscribe_count() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
        let shared = table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);
        assert!(table.set_max_msgs(1, 3), "the subscription is still there");

        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::QueueFull,
                ..
            }
        ));
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::QueueFull,
                ..
            }
        ));
        assert_eq!(shared.dropped.load(Ordering::Relaxed), 2);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Unknown,
            "three copies were written, the third was the last one asked for"
        );

        assert_eq!(received(&mut rx).sid, 1, "what was queued is still there");
        assert!(
            matches!(rx.try_recv(), Err(mpsc::error::TryRecvError::Disconnected)),
            "and then the reader sees the end, not an empty queue"
        );
    }

    /// The same count where the copy is refused by the connection's byte
    /// budget rather than by the subscription's own queue, and the log line
    /// for the drop that ends the subscription still names its subject.
    #[test]
    fn a_copy_over_the_byte_budget_counts_too_and_the_report_names_the_subject() {
        let one = message(1).queued_len();
        let budget = Budget::new(one);
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(8);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);
        assert!(table.set_max_msgs(1, 2));

        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::OverBudget,
                report: Some(DropReport { ref subject, .. })
            } if subject == b"a.b"
        ));
        assert_eq!(table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget), Delivered::Unknown);
        let _ = received(&mut rx);
        assert!(matches!(
            rx.try_recv(),
            Err(mpsc::error::TryRecvError::Disconnected)
        ));
    }

    /// A full queue costs one copy, not the connection, and the copy is
    /// counted where the subscription's owner can read it.
    #[test]
    fn a_full_queue_drops_and_counts_the_copy() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
        let shared = table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::QueueFull,
                report: Some(DropReport { dropped: 1, ref subject })
            } if subject == b"a.b"
        ));
        assert_eq!(shared.dropped.load(Ordering::Relaxed), 1);
        assert!(rx.try_recv().is_ok());
        assert!(
            rx.try_recv().is_err(),
            "the dropped copy is gone, and the subscription is still live"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false },
            "a drop is not a removal"
        );
    }

    /// A message that does not fit the byte budget is dropped and counted
    /// however many slots the queue has free, and the bytes of what was
    /// queued come back when the reader takes it.
    #[test]
    fn a_message_over_the_byte_budget_is_dropped_and_the_bytes_come_back() {
        let one = message(1).queued_len();
        let budget = Budget::new(one * 2);
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(100);
        let shared = table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);

        for _ in 0..2 {
            assert_eq!(
                table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
                Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
            );
        }
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::OverBudget,
                ..
            }
        ));
        assert_eq!(shared.dropped.load(Ordering::Relaxed), 1);

        // The reader takes one; the budget has room for exactly one more.
        let _ = received(&mut rx);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped { .. }
        ));
    }

    /// The budget is the connection's, not the subscription's: two readers
    /// that are both behind share it.
    #[test]
    fn the_budget_is_shared_by_every_subscription() {
        let one = message(1).queued_len();
        let budget = Budget::new(one);
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx1, _rx1) = mpsc::channel(100);
        let (tx2, _rx2) = mpsc::channel(100);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx1);
        table.insert(2, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx2);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            table.dispatch(2, message(2), &budget),
            Delivered::Dropped {
                reason: DropReason::OverBudget,
                ..
            }
        ));
    }

    /// A reader that has gone away takes its entry with it, so the driver can
    /// write the one `UNSUB` that is due.
    #[test]
    fn a_dropped_reader_removes_its_entry_once() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
        table.insert(1, b"a.b".to_vec(), None, tx);
        drop(rx);
        assert_eq!(table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget), Delivered::Gone);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget),
            Delivered::Unknown,
            "and only once"
        );
    }

    /// An entry whose reader went away with its `UNSUB` lost is swept without
    /// waiting for a message to find it, and the sweep names it so the
    /// `UNSUB` can be written.
    #[test]
    fn a_sweep_finds_the_readers_that_are_gone() {
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (live_tx, _live_rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
        let (gone_tx, gone_rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
        table.insert(1, b"a".to_vec(), None, live_tx);
        table.insert(2, b"b".to_vec(), None, gone_tx);
        drop(gone_rx);

        assert_eq!(table.take_closed(), vec![2]);
        assert!(table.take_closed().is_empty(), "swept once");
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(1, message(1), &roomy()),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false },
            "the live one is still there"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(2, message(2), &roomy()),
            Delivered::Unknown,
            "the swept one is not"
        );
    }

    /// The bound is the client's, because the `sid` is.
    #[test]
    fn the_table_is_bounded() {
        let mut table = Table::new(2);
        for sid in 0..2 {
            let (tx, _rx) = mpsc::channel(1);
            assert!(table.has_room());
            table.insert(sid, b"a".to_vec(), None, tx);
        }
        assert!(!table.has_room());
        assert_eq!(table.max(), 2);
        assert!(table.remove(0));
        assert!(table.has_room());
        assert!(!table.remove(0), "removing twice is not two removals");
    }

    fn named(subject: &[u8], queue_group: Option<&[u8]>, sid: Option<u64>) -> Named {
        Named {
            subject: subject.to_vec(),
            queue_group: queue_group.map(<[u8]>::to_vec),
            sid,
        }
    }

    fn refusal() -> Refusal {
        crate::refusal::classify(r#"Permissions Violation for Subscription to "secret.>""#)
            .expect("a refusal")
            .0
    }

    /// A refused subscription ends, its reader sees the end after what was
    /// queued, and it carries the reason; one nobody named is untouched.
    #[test]
    fn a_refused_subscription_ends_with_its_reason_and_others_do_not() {
        let budget = roomy();
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (tx1, mut rx1) = mpsc::channel(4);
        let (tx2, _rx2) = mpsc::channel(4);
        let refused = table.insert(1, b"secret.>".to_vec(), None, tx1);
        let other = table.insert(2, b"public.>".to_vec(), None, tx2);
        table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget);

        assert_eq!(table.refuse(&named(b"secret.>", None, None), &refusal()), 1);

        assert_eq!(refused.refusal.get(), Some(&refusal()));
        assert_eq!(other.refusal.get(), None);
        assert_eq!(
            received(&mut rx1).sid,
            1,
            "what was queued is still readable"
        );
        assert!(matches!(
            rx1.try_recv(),
            Err(mpsc::error::TryRecvError::Disconnected)
        ));
        assert_eq!(table.dispatch(1, message(1), &budget), Delivered::Unknown);
        assert_eq!(
            table.dispatch(2, message(2), &budget),
            Delivered::Ok { exhausted: false }
        );
    }

    /// The queue group is part of what the server named: a subscription on
    /// the same subject in another group is a different subscription, and a
    /// `sid` is exact whatever the subject says.
    #[test]
    fn a_refusal_is_matched_on_the_group_and_exactly_on_a_sid() {
        let mut table = Table::new(8);
        let (plain, _r1) = mpsc::channel(1);
        let (grouped, _r2) = mpsc::channel(1);
        let (twin, _r3) = mpsc::channel(1);
        table.insert(1, b"jobs".to_vec(), None, plain);
        table.insert(2, b"jobs".to_vec(), Some(b"workers".to_vec()), grouped);
        table.insert(3, b"jobs".to_vec(), Some(b"workers".to_vec()), twin);

        assert_eq!(
            table.refuse(&named(b"jobs", Some(b"workers"), Some(3)), &refusal()),
            1,
            "a sid names one subscription"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.refuse(&named(b"jobs", Some(b"workers"), None), &refusal()),
            1,
            "the other member of the group"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            table.refuse(&named(b"jobs", Some(b"workers"), None), &refusal()),
            0,
            "and only once"
        );
        assert!(table.remove(1), "the ungrouped one was never named");
    }
}
